<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Medina is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the second holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> migrated there in 622 and established the first Muslim community. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> until Ali moved his seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and later rulers including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> claimed its protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medina.jpg" alt="Medina" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konevi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Masjid_An-Nabawi_(Bird%27s_Eye_View).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By repelling the Mongols and eliminating the Crusader strongholds, it stabilised the region and became guardian of Mecca, <b>Medina</b> and Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu al-Hasan copied the Qur'an in his own hand four times, sending copies to <b>Medina</b>, Mecca and the Al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem; a fifth, begun at Bijaya for Hebron, was finished by his son after his dethronement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Selim I’s conquest of the Mamluk Sultanate in 1516–1517 brought Egypt, Syria and the holy cities of Mecca and <b>Medina</b> under Ottoman sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Prophet Muhammad died in <b>Medina</b> in June 632 CE without a universally acknowledged successor, and the uncertainty threatened to dissolve the young Muslim community before it had consolidated control over the Arabian Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As custodian of Mecca and <b>Medina</b> the kingdom stands at the centre of Islam, and its oil at the centre of the world economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1804 dan Fodio led his followers in a hijra, a migration explicitly modelled on the Prophet Muhammad's flight from Mecca to <b>Medina</b>, to Gudu, where his community acclaimed him Amir al-Mu'minin, Commander of the Faithful.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
